-- title:
--   Intermediate fields of a one-variable function field are curves
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra and $K$ algebraically closed, let $E$ be an intermediate field $K \subseteq E \subseteq F$, and let $t \in F$ be an element lying in $E$ and transcendental over $K$. Assume in addition that there is some $x \in F$, transcendental over $K$, such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the subfield $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Then three conclusions hold. First, $E$ satisfies [`AlgebraicCurve.IsCurveOver K E`](def/AlgebraicCurve_IsCurveOver.html#L15), that is: (a) every nonzero $f \in E$ admits a divisor $D$ on $E/K$ whose value at each place equals the order $v(f)$ and whose degree is $0$, where a place of $E/K$ means a valuation subring of $E$ containing the image of $K$, different from $E$ itself, and a principal ideal ring; (b) the residue field of every such place is a finite-dimensional $K$-module; and (c) the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega_{E/K}$ is free of rank $1$ over $E$. Second, $E$ is essentially of finite type over $K$. Third, there exists $y \in E$ transcendental over $K$ with $E$ finite-dimensional over $K(y)$.
--
--   This is the transcendence-degree-one case of the statement that a subextension of a finitely generated field extension is again finitely generated, in the form needed to recognise an intermediate field of a one-variable function field over an algebraically closed constant field as itself a one-variable function field. It is used when passing to subfields of function fields, for instance in the construction of period data on $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ and in the analysis of invariant subfields for Cerednik–Drinfeld quotients.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_isCurveOver_and_essFiniteType_intermediateField_of_transcendental_mem.lean

